WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.880
Why can one missing enzyme kill a child, when her parents, with half as much, are

2
00:00:04.880 --> 00:00:08.480
fine? The answer is a room with no way out.

3
00:00:11.483 --> 00:00:12.663
Start with a wall.

4
00:00:13.943 --> 00:00:17.663
A cell membrane is a double layer of lipids with an oily core.

5
00:00:19.123 --> 00:00:22.403
Small nonpolar molecules, like oxygen, slip straight through.

6
00:00:23.783 --> 00:00:26.263
Glucose and ions need doors the cell builds.

7
00:00:27.317 --> 00:00:30.037
Close that wall into a bag, and you have a room.

8
00:00:31.177 --> 00:00:37.097
The lysosome is the cell's recycling room, with digestive enzymes sealed inside, in

9
00:00:37.097 --> 00:00:40.557
acid. Old membrane is delivered in.

10
00:00:41.717 --> 00:00:45.717
The enzymes cut it into small parts, which leave through transporters.

11
00:00:46.717 --> 00:00:51.317
Now follow one lipid in that old membrane, GM2 ganglioside.

12
00:00:52.417 --> 00:00:57.137
One enzyme, HexA, snips one sugar off it, so its breakdown can go on.

13
00:00:58.657 --> 00:01:02.377
GM2's big sugar head cannot cross the lysosome's membrane.

14
00:01:03.577 --> 00:01:05.477
And no transporter carries it out.

15
00:01:06.450 --> 00:01:10.750
Nothing else in the cell does HexA's job well enough to stand in.

16
00:01:11.830 --> 00:01:15.190
So, in its simplest form, this room is a tap and a drain.

17
00:01:16.530 --> 00:01:19.690
What it holds is set by delivery in, against clearance out.

18
00:01:20.700 --> 00:01:23.380
Each parent has one working copy of the gene.

19
00:01:24.700 --> 00:01:27.160
Each makes about half the normal amount of HexA.

20
00:01:28.320 --> 00:01:29.340
So halve the drain.

21
00:01:30.700 --> 00:01:32.220
Does their lysosome fill up?

22
00:01:37.650 --> 00:01:42.870
No. A normal lysosome has far more HexA than it needs.

23
00:01:44.090 --> 00:01:47.190
It clears GM2 many times faster than it arrives.

24
00:01:48.130 --> 00:01:51.970
Halve the drain, and the level settles a little higher, but stays low.

25
00:01:53.050 --> 00:01:56.530
In the infantile form, there is almost no working enzyme.

26
00:01:57.870 --> 00:02:01.830
The drain is almost shut, but delivery goes on, from before birth.

27
00:02:03.470 --> 00:02:05.830
GM2 piles up, and the lysosomes swell.

28
00:02:07.210 --> 00:02:10.790
Yet the room stays intact, filling with something it cannot empty.

29
00:02:11.900 --> 00:02:15.060
Storage starts only when the drain falls below the tap.

30
00:02:16.440 --> 00:02:21.100
Measurements put that cut-off near 10 to 15% percent of normal, far below half.

31
00:02:22.580 --> 00:02:26.640
Below it, leftover enzyme slows the rise, so the illness starts later.

32
00:02:27.733 --> 00:02:29.513
Why are nerve cells hit hardest?

33
00:02:30.893 --> 00:02:34.693
Their membranes are rich in gangliosides, so delivery is high.

34
00:02:36.033 --> 00:02:37.393
And neurons do not divide.

35
00:02:38.653 --> 00:02:42.333
A dividing cell splits its stored GM2 between its daughters.

36
00:02:43.673 --> 00:02:46.613
A neuron cannot dilute stored GM2 that way.

37
00:02:47.633 --> 00:02:51.253
So a sealed room keeps whatever reaches it and is not cleared.

38
00:02:52.653 --> 00:02:55.233
Half a drain still keeps up, so the parents are fine.

39
00:02:56.533 --> 00:03:00.613
With almost no drain, the room fills, and nerve cells are hit hardest.
